WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.940
An adult human brain has about 86 billion neurons.

2
00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:11.720
The genome that builds it has about 20,000 genes that code for proteins.

3
00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:16.920
Each neuron connects to many others, so the brain carries on the order of 100

4
00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:17.920
trillion synapses.

5
00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.580
The genes are fewer than the neurons by a factor of millions.

6
00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:24.760
They are fewer than the synapses by a factor of billions.

7
00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:27.760
How does the smaller number wire the larger one?

8
00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:34.510
Labels are cheap. Take 72 genes, and let each one be either on or off in a cell.

9
00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:37.710
Every extra gene doubles the number of combinations.

10
00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:42.950
72 genes give about 4.7 × 10²¹ to the 21st labels.

11
00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:46.350
That is more than ten billion times the number of neurons.

12
00:00:47.533 --> 00:00:49.253
But a label is not a wire.

13
00:00:49.933 --> 00:00:51.953
A tag says which cell a neuron is.

14
00:00:52.473 --> 00:00:56.613
It says nothing about which of the other 86 billion cells it should connect to.

15
00:00:57.313 --> 00:01:01.993
A list with one instruction for each synapse would be billions of times longer than

16
00:01:01.993 --> 00:01:02.993
the list of genes.

17
00:01:03.413 --> 00:01:07.753
So the information has to be compressed into rules that apply in many places at

18
00:01:07.753 --> 00:01:08.753
once.

19
00:01:09.017 --> 00:01:13.797
The first rule is a gradient, a molecule whose amount rises smoothly across a

20
00:01:13.797 --> 00:01:18.337
tissue. Picture a receptor that is high at one edge of the retina and low at the

21
00:01:18.337 --> 00:01:22.717
other. The target holds a matching gradient of a cue that the receptor reads.

22
00:01:23.497 --> 00:01:28.417
Each growth cone, the tip of an axon, stops where the signal it senses reaches a set

23
00:01:28.417 --> 00:01:33.637
level. One receptor gene and one cue gene place thousands of axons in order.

24
00:01:34.667 --> 00:01:37.607
The second rule is to overproduce, then select.

25
00:01:38.327 --> 00:01:41.347
Let each axon branch over a wider area than it needs.

26
00:01:42.027 --> 00:01:44.387
Neighbouring retinal cells tend to fire together.

27
00:01:44.767 --> 00:01:49.027
So keep the branches whose activity matches that of their neighbours, and remove the

28
00:01:49.027 --> 00:01:54.247
rest. The tissue measures this after the connections form, so the genome never has

29
00:01:54.247 --> 00:01:55.247
to store it.

30
00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:57.760
Now block activity in the eye.

31
00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:01.460
In regrowing goldfish axons, the coarse order survived.

32
00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:03.440
The fine precision did not.

33
00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:06.440
Zebrafish embryos still mapped precisely.

34
00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:10.820
So gradients place the axons roughly, and activity sharpens them.

35
00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:14.480
The genome stores rules, and the tissue measures the rest.
